export const faqs=[
  ['What is Litbuy’s role in the shopping process?','Litbuy describes its service as a shopping-agent and parcel-forwarding app. In practical terms, it is a route for submitting a product link, managing warehouse-stage checks and arranging an international parcel; it is not the original marketplace seller.'],
  ['Which Chinese marketplaces can a Litbuy order start from?','Litbuy’s public app descriptions name Taobao, 1688 and Weidian, while also referring to other Chinese marketplaces. A product link still needs to be checked in the order flow for availability, options and seller-specific restrictions.'],
  ['How does a buyer start an order?','Litbuy says buyers can paste a product link, choose the relevant options and place an order. Before submitting, record the exact colour, size, version and quantity you want; similar-looking variants can have different prices or stock.'],
  ['Does Litbuy provide QC photos?','Litbuy states that it provides real warehouse QC photos before shipping. Treat those photos as a decision point: compare the visible item, colour, logo placement, construction and any stated measurements with the option you selected.'],
  ['What does “Pack First, Pay Later” mean?','Litbuy says buyers can preview a parcel’s actual weight and size before paying the shipping fee. That makes the packing stage the right time to review the selected parcel, not the moment to assume an earlier estimate is final.'],
  ['Can several orders be sent together?','Litbuy lists consolidation and extra packaging among its features. Combining orders can make parcel planning simpler, but it also means the final parcel’s size, weight and route should be reviewed before you pay for shipping.'],
  ['Can I track a dispatched parcel?','Litbuy advertises real-time parcel tracking with logistics updates. Tracking is useful for following a parcel after dispatch, but it does not turn a carrier estimate into a guaranteed delivery date.'],
  ['Which payment methods are listed?','Litbuy’s public listing mentions PayPal and bank cards, with additional options described as coming later. Check the live checkout for the methods, currency conversion and any charges available to your specific account and destination.'],
  ['Where can I confirm returns, refunds or a shipping quote?','These details depend on the seller, the selected item, the destination and the current order screen. Do not infer a return right, a shipping price or a delivery time from a general guide; confirm the current policy and order-specific information before payment.']
];

const article=`
<span class="eyebrow">LITBUY RESEARCH NOTE / WORKFLOW GUIDE</span>
<h1 class="page-title">How to use Litbuy with fewer assumptions: a practical link, QC and parcel workflow.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">Litbuy publicly presents itself as a China-shopping agent and parcel-forwarding service. This guide separates the platform features it lists from the decisions a buyer still needs to make for each seller, item and shipping route.</p>
<article class="research-article">
<p class="research-note">Research basis: Litbuy’s current public app descriptions list link-based ordering from Taobao, 1688 and Weidian; warehouse QC photos; consolidation and extra packaging; a “Pack First, Pay Later” parcel stage; tracking; and listed payment methods. Features, prices, routes and seller policies can change, so the live order screen remains the final source for a particular purchase.</p>

<h2>1. Start with a product link, not a vague product name</h2>
<p>Litbuy describes a simple starting action: paste a product link, select the options and place the order. That is useful only when the link and the chosen variant are precise. A marketplace listing can contain several colours, batches, size ranges, add-ons or seller-defined versions under one title. “The black jacket” is not enough information if the listing has two black materials, a numbered batch and a separate sizing chart.</p>
<p>Before you submit anything, save the link and write a short order note for yourself: the exact option name, size, quantity and the price shown at that moment. If the page has a measurement chart, compare it with a garment that already fits rather than relying on a familiar letter size. This is not a Litbuy-specific rule; it is the basic discipline that makes a link-based buying workflow easier to audit later. It also gives you something concrete to compare when the order page, QC photos or parcel contents appear.</p>

<h2>2. Treat the agent step and the seller step as different things</h2>
<p>Litbuy’s own description frames the service as a shopping agent and forwarding tool. The original item still belongs to a marketplace seller, and seller-side details can remain uncertain until the order is reviewed. Availability, variation names, seller shipping, return eligibility and item-specific instructions are not universal platform promises. They are reasons to read the original listing carefully before moving forward.</p>
<p>A practical way to avoid confusion is to make two checks. First, ask whether the linked listing is the item you actually want. Second, ask whether the option selected in the agent order matches the listing. Do not compress those into one glance. If a seller changes a price, removes a variation or sends a message about stock, you want to know which stage changed and why. Clear records—screenshots, selected option text and the item link—are more useful than memory when an order has several similar pieces.</p>

<h2>3. Use warehouse QC photos as evidence, not as decoration</h2>
<p>Litbuy says it provides real warehouse QC photos before shipping. That feature is valuable because it creates a pause between purchase and international dispatch. The best use of that pause is not simply asking whether the item “looks good.” Instead, compare the photos against the information that mattered when you ordered: the colourway, visible materials, labels, hardware, print alignment, pair or set consistency, and the stated size or measurement information when it is available.</p>
<p>Photos also have limits. They show what is visible under one set of lighting and angles; they do not prove every material property, future durability or fit on a particular person. For clothing and footwear, a labelled size is not a substitute for measurements. For electronics, a product image does not prove full compatibility with your charger, region or intended use. If an important detail cannot be checked from the listing or images, slow down before shipping rather than converting uncertainty into an assumption.</p>

<h2>4. Warehouse planning is where a list becomes a parcel</h2>
<p>Litbuy lists consolidation and extra packaging as part of its service. Consolidation means several purchases can be considered together for one parcel. That can be practical, but it changes the decision from “Do I want this item?” to “How should these items travel together?” Shoes, outerwear, fragile objects and small accessories do not create the same packing needs. A buyer who plans at the warehouse stage can decide whether the parcel should stay focused, whether a delicate item needs more protection and whether another item should wait for a later shipment.</p>
<p>There is no universal best parcel size. A larger parcel may spread some fixed handling steps across more items, but it can also be heavier, larger or subject to a different shipping calculation. A small parcel can be simpler to inspect and track, but it may not be the most efficient choice for every route. The useful habit is to plan by item type and destination, then compare the actual packed parcel information instead of assuming that one order strategy works for all purchases.</p>

<h2>5. “Pack First, Pay Later” changes the shipping conversation</h2>
<p>Litbuy describes a “Pack First, Pay Later” flow in which the actual weight and size of a parcel can be viewed before the shipping fee is paid. This is one of the clearest places to avoid false certainty. A product page price and an early shipping estimate are not the same thing as a final international shipping charge. Packing can change the parcel’s measured weight, dimensions and the practical route choices available for the destination.</p>
<p>When the packed parcel is ready, review the items included, the destination, the displayed measurements and the shipping choices available in the order screen. Read the label of each line rather than choosing only by a name that sounds fast or cheap. If a route lists limits, special handling, insurance options or restricted-item conditions, treat those as order-specific information to examine carefully. Paying after the parcel information is visible is useful precisely because it gives you a moment to decide with better data.</p>

<h2>6. Tracking is a record of movement, not a delivery promise</h2>
<p>Litbuy advertises real-time parcel tracking with logistics updates. Tracking can help you see dispatch events and follow the stages reported by a carrier. It does not guarantee a delivery date, resolve customs questions or prove that a scan will update on a fixed schedule. International parcels can move through handoffs, flights, destination processing and local delivery systems that each update differently.</p>
<p>Use tracking calmly. Save the number, check the carrier events in context and avoid treating a short pause between scans as proof that something has gone wrong. If an update conflicts with the address, parcel status or timeline shown in your order, use the platform’s support route with the order number and the relevant screenshots. Specific information is easier to investigate than a general message that a package is “late.”</p>

<h2>7. Check checkout details where they are actually shown</h2>
<p>Litbuy’s public app listing mentions PayPal and bank cards, while describing more payment options as forthcoming. That tells you which methods may be offered; it does not guarantee that every method, fee, currency conversion or promotion will apply to every country and account. The practical rule is simple: read the live checkout before you approve it. Confirm the currency, amount, selected items and payment method, and retain the payment record.</p>
<p>The same restraint applies to discounts, shipping claims and comparisons. A banner, a community post or an old review is not an order confirmation. Use current figures from the checkout and parcel screen. If a number matters to your decision, take a screenshot before payment. This is how you keep the difference clear between a platform feature, a current quotation and a promise that has actually been made for your order.</p>

<h2>8. A reliable first-order routine</h2>
<p>A careful first order does not need to be complicated. Choose a clear link; select the exact option; record the listing details; inspect the warehouse QC photos; decide whether to consolidate; review the packed weight and dimensions; choose a shipping option only after reading its current details; and keep the order and tracking information together. This sequence is useful because each decision is made at the stage where the relevant information becomes available.</p>
<p>Litbuy’s public feature set is built around these stages—link ordering, QC, parcel preparation, payment for shipping and tracking. The buyer’s job is to resist filling gaps with assumptions. Do not assume stock, fit, shipping cost, delivery timing or returns from a general description. Verify what matters on the specific listing and the current order page. That approach is slower than an impulse checkout, but it is usually the clearest way to make a cross-border shopping workflow manageable.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The most useful way to evaluate a shopping agent is not as a magic shortcut, but as a sequence of checkpoints. Litbuy publicly describes tools for starting with a marketplace link, reviewing warehouse QC photos, consolidating items, paying shipping after the packed parcel is measured and following tracking updates. Those tools can improve visibility. They do not remove the need to read the seller listing, compare the selected option, verify the live shipping details or keep records. Use every checkpoint, and let the current order information—not an assumption—make the final decision.</p>
</article>`;

export function mount(){
  const path=location.pathname.replace(/\/$/,'')||'/';
  if(path==='/journal'){
    const main=document.querySelector('main');
    if(main&&!main.dataset.litbuyResearch){main.dataset.litbuyResearch='true';main.innerHTML=`<section class="page-hero research-hero">${article}</section>`}
  }
  if(path==='/faq'){
    const main=document.querySelector('main');
    if(main&&!main.dataset.litbuyResearch){main.dataset.litbuyResearch='true';main.innerHTML=`<section class="page-hero research-hero"><span class="eyebrow">LITBUY RESEARCH FAQ</span><h1 class="page-title">Questions worth checking before you place a Litbuy order.</h1><p class="page-lead">The answers below use Litbuy’s public feature descriptions and distinguish them from seller-specific or route-specific details that must be confirmed in the live order flow.</p></section><section class="faq research-faq">${faqs.map((item,index)=>`<details${index===0?' open':''}><summary>${item[0]}</summary><p>${item[1]}</p></details>`).join('')}</section>`}
  }
  if(!document.querySelector('#litbuy-research-style')){
    const style=document.createElement('style');style.id='litbuy-research-style';style.textContent=`.research-hero{padding-bottom:38px}.research-article{max-width:820px;margin:44px auto 0}.research-article h2{margin:46px 0 13px;color:#fff;font:800 30px/1.08 Manrope,Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.06em}.research-article p{margin:0 0 18px;color:#d6cfdb;font-size:16px;line-height:1.72}.research-article .research-note{padding:16px 18px;border-left:3px solid #A855F7;background:#1A1421;color:#e6d8f1;font-size:14px}.research-faq{max-width:920px;margin:0 auto 72px}.research-faq details{border-color:var(--line)}.research-faq summary{font-size:18px}.research-faq p{line-height:1.65}@media(max-width:860px){.research-hero{padding-bottom:18px}.research-article{margin-top:28px}.research-article h2{margin-top:34px;font-size:24px}.research-article p{font-size:15px;line-height:1.62}.research-faq{margin-bottom:48px}.research-faq summary{font-size:16px}}`;document.head.append(style);
  }
}
